emu-shm-sem-flags.patch — the fix for the fifth wall, as a patch.

WHY THIS IS A PATCH FILE AND NOT A COMMIT
-----------------------------------------
The three files it touches are NOT in this repository. They live in the
`src/external/xnu` submodule (a checkout of darling-xnu), and this repository
only carries a gitlink to it. The on-disk checkout of that submodule is at
fa29287aa2f0115271e091f1031f53c9e024005d while pr-arm64 records
12132d9f9f2dc09ae0a9129a925299ce1f1a81c5, and no build script here compiles
the emulation at all — the shim in the overlay
(usr/lib/system/libsystem_kernel.dylib) is a prebuilt artefact. So the fix
cannot be committed to pr-arm64, and it cannot be tested end to end from this
machine. See WORKAROUND-344.md §13.

Apply it from a checkout of darling-xnu at or near the recorded gitlink:

    git apply emu-shm-sem-flags.patch

or, to see it first, `git apply --stat` / `--check`.

WHAT IT CHANGES
---------------
  1. xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/shm_open.c
     Drops oflags_bsd_to_linux() on the way to elfcalls()->shm_open. That
     slot is the HOST's shm_open (src/startup/mldr/elfcalls/elfcalls.c:118),
     so it takes BSD flags. Translating them turned the caller's O_CREAT into
     a bit the host reads as O_ASYNC, and every call carrying O_CREAT failed
     with EINVAL regardless of the name.

  2. xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/sem_open.c
     The same line, in the neighbouring POSIX slot.

  3. linux_premigration/ext/for-libelfloader.c
     wd, which is the dirfd argument of openat(2), was being assigned the
     translated open flags. It is now get_perthread_wd(), the form the
     correct function two lines below in the same file already uses.

DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED
------------------------
impl/fcntl/openat.c, impl/fcntl/fcntl.c and linux_premigration/ext/
file_handle.c also call oflags_bsd_to_linux, and they are CORRECT: they feed
a Linux syscall through LINUX_SYSCALL, which is a different contract. A sweep
that removed those would break three working paths. The rule is not "stop
translating" but "do not translate on the way to a host function filled into
the elfcalls table".

HOW TO CHECK IT WITHOUT A GUEST
--------------------------------
    sh build-freebsd/shm-flags-contract.sh

That prints what the host's shm_open answers for each of these values, and it is
the oracle: after the fix, the guest's shm_open must agree with it.

diff --git a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/linux_premigration/ext/for-libelfloader.c b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/linux_premigration/ext/for-libelfloader.c
index dcc07ea..a7bbfc0 100644
--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/linux_premigration/ext/for-libelfloader.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/linux_premigration/ext/for-libelfloader.c
@@ -16,7 +16,13 @@ long _open_for_libelfloader(const char* path, int flags, unsigned int mode) {
 	CANCELATION_POINT();
 
 	linux_flags = oflags_bsd_to_linux(flags);
-	wd = oflags_bsd_to_linux(flags);
+	// wd is the dirfd argument of openat(2) and has nothing to do with the
+	// open flags. This line gave it the flags, so the syscall was called
+	// with a flag bit in the dirfd slot — for O_RDWR|O_CREAT|O_EXCL that is
+	// openat(194, …), which is a descriptor nobody opened. The correct form
+	// is in _access_for_libelfloader below, and in every other openat
+	// wrapper in this tree.
+	wd = get_perthread_wd();
 
 	if (sizeof(void*) == 4) {
 		linux_flags |= LINUX_O_LARGEFILE;
diff --git a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/sem_open.c b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/sem_open.c
index ba19a14..c04a2b5 100644
--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/sem_open.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/sem_open.c
@@ -19,8 +19,13 @@ long sys_sem_open(const char* name, int oflag, int mode, int value)
 	int* ptr;
 	
 	// __simple_printf("sem_open %s, %d, %d, %d\n", name, oflag, mode, value);
-	
-	ptr = elfcalls()->sem_open(name, oflags_bsd_to_linux(oflag), mode, value);
+
+	// The flags go down as they came: elfcalls()->sem_open is the HOST's
+	// sem_open, filled from the host's symbols (elfcalls.c:118), and it
+	// takes BSD flags. Translating them here is the same defect that kept
+	// shm_open's pool from ever existing, in the neighbouring POSIX slot —
+	// see WORKAROUND-344.md §12 and the fix in wrapped/shm_open.c.
+	ptr = elfcalls()->sem_open(name, oflag, mode, value);
 	//__simple_printf("sem_open -> %p\n", ptr);
 	
 	if (!ptr)
diff --git a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/shm_open.c b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/shm_open.c
index 471c978..f334cc9 100644
--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/shm_open.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/wrapped/shm_open.c
@@ -17,7 +17,25 @@ long sys_shm_open(const char* name, int oflag, int mode)
 #ifndef VARIANT_DYLD
 	int ret;
 
-	ret = elfcalls()->shm_open(name, oflags_bsd_to_linux(oflag), mode);
+	/* The flags go down AS THEY CAME. Do not translate them.
+	 *
+	 * elfcalls()->shm_open is the HOST's shm_open — mldr fills that table
+	 * from the host's own symbols (src/startup/mldr/elfcalls/elfcalls.c:118,
+	 * into the slot declared at elfcalls.h:51) — so it takes BSD flags.
+	 * Translating them here converted the caller's O_CREAT into a bit the
+	 * host reads as O_ASYNC, and every call carrying O_CREAT then failed
+	 * with EINVAL no matter what the name was:
+	 *
+	 *     caller 0x202 -> host got 0x042 -> EINVAL
+	 *     caller 0xa02 -> host got 0x0c2 -> EINVAL
+	 *
+	 * which is the whole of why the window probe's shm pool never came
+	 * into being. See WORKAROUND-344.md §12 and §13.
+	 *
+	 * The translation is still needed on the path that reaches a Linux
+	 * syscall — impl/fcntl/* goes to LINUX_SYSCALL and that one is
+	 * correct. This is a different contract and only this one is wrong. */
+	ret = elfcalls()->shm_open(name, oflag, mode);
 	if (ret == -1)
 		ret = -errno_linux_to_bsd(elfcalls()->get_errno());
 
